'''''Pontederia sagittata''''' is a [[species]] of [[aquatic plant]] belonging to the family [[Pontederiaceae]].<ref name=POWO_698184-1/>

==Description==
It is a [[perennial plant]] with erect, [[Rhizome|rhizomatous]] tough stems. Basal leaves, emerging; [[Pod (fruit)|pods]] 10–27&nbsp;cm, the apex emarginate; petioles up to 1 m, not inflated, sometimes reddish; Blades 8-32 x 5–19&nbsp;cm, lanceolate, the base cordate, the apex acute. Inflorescence with more than 50 flowers; flowers arranged in groups of 3, 4 or more along the peduncle, sessile; peduncle (6-)10-20(-22) cm, thick, hairy to hairy or rarely glabrous; internode between spathes (3.5-)5-16(-20) cm; blade of lower [[spathe]] 8-32 x 5–19&nbsp;cm; upper spathe (3-)4-7(-9) cm, open on the upper 1/2, the apex mucronulate. 1.5&nbsp;cm flowers. [[Perianth]] lilac or rarely white, externally hairy-glandular; outer lobes c. 6mm; internal lobes c. 6mm Long filaments 6–9&nbsp;mm, with glandular hairs in the upper 1/3, short ones 0.7-3.5&nbsp;mm, with glandular hairs in the upper 1/3; anthers of upper [[whorl]] 0.75–1&nbsp;mm, yellow; anthers of lower whorl 0.55-0.85&nbsp;mm, yellow. Pistil 3–6&nbsp;mm; style hairy towards apex; stigma 3-lobed. [[Achene#Types|Utricle]]s 5–6&nbsp;mm, deltiform, with smooth rib edges; seeds 2.3-2.5&nbsp;mm, smooth, obovoid, covered by a mucilaginous layer with an oily appearance.{{citation needed|date=January 2024}}
[[File:Pontederia sagittata02.jpg|thumb|center|Inflorescence]]

==Taxonomy==
''Pontederia sagittata'' was first described by [[Carl Borivoj Presl|Carl Presl]] in 1827. It has sometimes been reduced to a form or variety of ''[[Pontederia cordata]]''.<ref name=POWO_698184-1/>

==Distribution and habitat==
''Pontederia sagittata'' has a discontinuous distribution, being [[Native species|native]] from Mexico to Central America and then eastern and southern [[Brazil]].<ref name=POWO_698184-1/> It is found on the edges of rivers, lagoons, ponds, and swamps,<ref>T (Novelo 187, BM); Ch (Breedlove 29040, DUKE); Y (Lot and Novelo 880, MEXU); G (Lundell 1445, MICH); H (Barkley and Hernández 40443, GH); CR (Pittier 10301, US); P (Croat 9832, MO).</ref> at altitudes of up to {{cvt|1500|m|ft}}.{{citation needed|date=January 2024}}

